WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:07.640
We went to a school and noted the heights of a very large set of students.

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:10.260
Let's make a line to denote their heights.

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:13.100
The heights recorded were in meters.

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:18.040
The heights recorded were between '0.9' and '2.1' meters.

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:22.020
Let's mark heights with intervals of '0.2' meters.

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:26.820
Like 1.1, 1.3, 1.5 and so on.

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:32.060
If we plot the number of students on this axis, then this is the data we get.

00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:35.040
Maximum at the '1.5' meters line.

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:40.240
Equally distributed on both sides of '1.5', which is the central value.

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:42.560
This point is the maximum.

00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:46.780
This type of distribution is called normal distribution

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:51.380
and the data is distributed equally on both sides of the central value.

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:56.800
It Is also referred to as the bell curve as its shape resembles a bell.

00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:59.900
But is the data always normally distributed?

00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:03.900
Does it always look symmetrical along the central value line?

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:06.540
We will look at an example to understand this.

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:09.640
We wanted to look at the graph of income distribution

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:11.760
per annum in a particular region.

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:14.640
Say we have the incomes on this line.

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:17.500
Let the income be in thousands of dollars.

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:21.540
The range going from 10,000 to a 100,000 dollars .

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:24.520
Let's zoom in to look at the data closely.

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:26.220
This is what we found.

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:31.400
Majority of the people earn somewhere between '20,000' and '50,000' dollars.

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:35.940
Very few earn less than '10,000' dollars and very few earn more than

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:37.520
'100,000' dollars.

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:41.380
Here, the central value is around '50,000' dollars.

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:43.480
Look at the figure closely.

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:47.360
We can see a long tail on the right-hand side of the central value.

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:50.180
Yes, it surely looks like a tail.

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:53.400
But there's something important we need to know here.

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:56.880
As the tail is on the positive side of the central value,

00:01:56.900 --> 00:02:00.560
we say that the distribution is positively skewed.

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:04.440
It is not equally distributed on both sides of the Central value.

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:06.180
This is skewness.

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:09.200
If we compare it to the normal distribution curve.

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:11.500
we can clearly see the difference.

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:13.920
Then what is negative Skewness?

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:15.980
To understand negative Skewness,

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:18.280
let's take a very simple example.

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:22.480
We have the data of the marks scored by students in an easy test.

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:25.620
Let the marks range from '20 to 80'.

00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:29.600
We zoom in to understand the data we gathered.

00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:33.160
This time the distribution looked something like this.

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:35.960
This is the distribution of marks of students.

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:39.460
Majority of the students scored between '50 and 80',

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:41.980
while the central value is '50'.

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:43.920
We see a tail here as well.

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:48.080
But this time it lies on the left hand side of the central value.

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:51.720
Because it is skewed towards the left hand side of the central value,

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:55.140
we can say that the data is negatively skewed.

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:58.440
Now let's zoom out to see all three distributions.

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:00.660
In the normal distributions case,

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:05.180
the data is distributed equally on both sides of the central value.

00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:07.520
In cases where the data is skewed

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:10.340
the distribution is not equal on both sides

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:13.540
If the tail is towards the right side of the central value,

00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:16.020
we say that it's positively skewed.

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:21.760
And if it's towards the left it's negatively skewed.

